WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.036 --> 00:02.536 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference March 16,

00:02.536 --> 00:03.816 align:middle
2022 CHAIR POWELL.

00:04.626 --> 00:04.996 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:04.996 --> 00:07.536 align:middle
I want to begin by acknowledging
the tremendous hardship the

00:07.626 --> 00:09.186 align:middle
Ukrainian people are suffering

00:09.396 --> 00:10.986 align:middle
as a result of Russia's
invasion.

00:11.916 --> 00:13.266 align:middle
The human toll is tragic.

00:14.416 --> 00:16.366 align:middle
The financial and
economic implications

00:16.366 --> 00:18.636 align:middle
for the global economy
and the U.S. economy are

00:18.636 --> 00:19.476 align:middle
highly uncertain.

00:20.906 --> 00:23.616 align:middle
At the Federal Reserve,
we are strongly committed

00:23.616 --> 00:25.576 align:middle
to achieving the
monetary policy goals

00:25.576 --> 00:26.746 align:middle
that Congress has given us:

00:27.276 --> 00:29.186 align:middle
maximum employment
and price stability.

00:30.356 --> 00:31.886 align:middle
Today, in support
of these goals,

00:32.396 --> 00:35.166 align:middle
the FOMC raised its
policy interest rate

00:35.226 --> 00:36.696 align:middle
by ¼ percentage point.

00:37.776 --> 00:40.496 align:middle
The economy is very strong,
and against the backdrop

00:40.496 --> 00:43.226 align:middle
of an extremely tight labor
market and high inflation,

00:43.896 --> 00:46.376 align:middle
the Committee anticipates
that ongoing increases

00:46.376 --> 00:48.906 align:middle
in the target range for
the federal funds rate will

00:48.906 --> 00:49.506 align:middle
be appropriate.

00:51.086 --> 00:53.496 align:middle
In addition, we expect to
begin reducing the size

00:53.496 --> 00:55.076 align:middle
of our balance sheet
at a coming meeting.

00:56.986 --> 00:58.606 align:middle
Economic activity expanded

00:58.606 --> 01:03.336 align:middle
at a robust 5½ percent pace
last year, reflecting progress

01:03.336 --> 01:05.566 align:middle
on vaccinations and the
reopening of the economy,

01:06.116 --> 01:07.936 align:middle
fiscal and monetary
policy support,

01:07.936 --> 01:10.356 align:middle
and the healthy financial
positions

01:10.356 --> 01:11.896 align:middle
of households and businesses.

01:13.276 --> 01:16.646 align:middle
The rapid spread of the Omicron
variant led to some slowing

01:16.646 --> 01:18.416 align:middle
in economic activity
early this year.

01:19.236 --> 01:21.936 align:middle
But cases have declined
sharply since mid-January,

01:22.526 --> 01:24.886 align:middle
and the slowdown seems to
have been mild and brief.

01:26.346 --> 01:27.706 align:middle
Although the invasion of Ukraine

01:27.706 --> 01:30.846 align:middle
and related events represent
a downside risk to the outlook

01:30.846 --> 01:34.426 align:middle
for economic activity,
FOMC participants continue

01:34.426 --> 01:35.876 align:middle
to foresee solid growth.

01:36.966 --> 01:39.656 align:middle
As shown in our Summary
of Economic Projections,

01:40.146 --> 01:43.046 align:middle
the median projection for
real GDP growth stands

01:43.046 --> 01:47.126 align:middle
at 2.8 percent this year,
2.2 percent next year,

01:47.496 --> 01:49.636 align:middle
and 2 percent in 2024.

01:51.636 --> 01:53.776 align:middle
The labor market has
continued to strengthen

01:54.096 --> 01:55.326 align:middle
and is extremely tight.

01:56.406 --> 01:58.816 align:middle
Over the first two months
of the year, employment rose

01:58.816 --> 02:00.316 align:middle
by more than a million jobs.

02:01.186 --> 02:04.476 align:middle
In February, the unemployment
rate hit a post-pandemic low

02:04.476 --> 02:07.636 align:middle
of 3.8 percent, a
bit below the median

02:07.636 --> 02:09.376 align:middle
of Committee participants'
estimates

02:09.436 --> 02:11.176 align:middle
of its longer-run normal level.

02:13.056 --> 02:15.956 align:middle
The improvements in labor market
conditions have been widespread,

02:16.136 --> 02:17.746 align:middle
including for workers
at the lower end

02:17.746 --> 02:19.506 align:middle
of the wage distribution as well

02:19.506 --> 02:21.426 align:middle
as for African Americans
and Hispanics.

02:22.516 --> 02:23.996 align:middle
Labor demand is very strong,

02:24.246 --> 02:26.616 align:middle
and while labor force
participation has increased

02:26.616 --> 02:29.466 align:middle
somewhat, labor supply
remains subdued.

02:29.616 --> 02:33.226 align:middle
As a result, employers are
having difficulties filling job

02:33.226 --> 02:35.496 align:middle
openings, and wages are rising

02:35.496 --> 02:37.626 align:middle
at their fastest
pace in many years.

02:39.036 --> 02:42.186 align:middle
FOMC participants expect the
labor market to remain strong,

02:42.746 --> 02:45.536 align:middle
with the median projection for
the unemployment rate declining

02:45.536 --> 02:49.476 align:middle
to 3.5 percent by the end of
this year and remaining near

02:49.476 --> 02:50.946 align:middle
that level thereafter.

02:52.606 --> 02:56.066 align:middle
Inflation remains well above our
longer-run goal of 2 percent.

02:57.016 --> 02:59.316 align:middle
Aggregate demand is
strong, and bottlenecks

02:59.316 --> 03:02.026 align:middle
and supply constraints are
limiting how quickly production

03:02.026 --> 03:02.726 align:middle
can respond.

03:03.976 --> 03:06.856 align:middle
These supply disruptions have
been larger, and longer lasting,

03:06.856 --> 03:10.476 align:middle
than anticipated, exacerbated
by waves of the virus here

03:10.476 --> 03:13.876 align:middle
and abroad, and price pressures
have spread to a broader range

03:13.876 --> 03:15.016 align:middle
of goods and services.

03:15.896 --> 03:18.146 align:middle
Additionally, higher
energy prices are driving

03:18.146 --> 03:19.656 align:middle
up overall inflation.

03:20.656 --> 03:23.406 align:middle
The surge in prices of crude
oil and other commodities

03:23.406 --> 03:25.076 align:middle
that resulted from
Russia's invasion

03:25.076 --> 03:28.226 align:middle
of Ukraine will put
additional upward pressure

03:28.226 --> 03:30.236 align:middle
on near-term inflation
here at home.

03:31.596 --> 03:34.316 align:middle
We understand that high
inflation imposes significant

03:34.316 --> 03:37.046 align:middle
hardship, especially
on those least able

03:37.046 --> 03:39.756 align:middle
to meet the higher costs
of essentials like food,

03:39.996 --> 03:41.346 align:middle
housing, and transportation.

03:42.396 --> 03:43.826 align:middle
We know that the
best thing we can do

03:43.826 --> 03:45.536 align:middle
to support a strong
labor market is

03:45.536 --> 03:48.896 align:middle
to promote a long expansion-and
that is only possible

03:48.896 --> 03:50.806 align:middle
in an environment
of price stability.

03:52.046 --> 03:53.916 align:middle
As we emphasize in
our policy statement,

03:54.546 --> 03:57.116 align:middle
with appropriate firming in
the stance of monetary policy,

03:57.556 --> 03:59.066 align:middle
we expect inflation to return

03:59.066 --> 04:01.826 align:middle
to 2 percent while the
labor market remains strong.

04:03.146 --> 04:05.996 align:middle
That said, inflation is likely
to take longer to return

04:05.996 --> 04:08.486 align:middle
to our price-stability goal
than previously expected.

04:09.576 --> 04:11.076 align:middle
The median inflation projection

04:11.076 --> 04:14.736 align:middle
of FOMC participants is 4.3
percent this year and falls

04:14.736 --> 04:18.606 align:middle
to 2.7 percent next year
and 2.3 percent in 2024;

04:19.636 --> 04:21.906 align:middle
this trajectory is notably
higher than projected

04:21.906 --> 04:25.056 align:middle
in December, and participants
continue to see risks

04:25.056 --> 04:26.346 align:middle
as weighted to the upside.

04:28.376 --> 04:31.736 align:middle
The Fed's monetary policy
actions have been guided

04:31.736 --> 04:34.686 align:middle
by our mandate to
promote maximum employment

04:34.996 --> 04:37.066 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people.

04:37.336 --> 04:39.796 align:middle
Our policy has been adapting

04:39.796 --> 04:41.646 align:middle
to the evolving economic
environment,

04:41.836 --> 04:43.146 align:middle
and it will continue to do so.

04:44.046 --> 04:46.296 align:middle
As I noted, the Committee
raised the target range

04:46.296 --> 04:50.696 align:middle
for the federal funds rate by ¼
percentage point and anticipates

04:50.696 --> 04:51.886 align:middle
that ongoing increases

04:51.886 --> 04:53.546 align:middle
in the target range
will be appropriate.

04:54.806 --> 04:56.886 align:middle
The median projection
for the appropriate level

04:56.886 --> 04:59.986 align:middle
of the federal funds rate
is 1.9 percent at the end

04:59.986 --> 05:02.206 align:middle
of this year-a full
percentage point higher

05:02.206 --> 05:03.396 align:middle
than projected in December.

05:04.426 --> 05:05.736 align:middle
Over the following two years,

05:05.736 --> 05:09.126 align:middle
the median projection is
2.8 percent-somewhat higher

05:09.126 --> 05:11.556 align:middle
than the median estimate
of its longer-run value.

05:12.576 --> 05:14.996 align:middle
Of course, these projections
do not represent a Committee

05:14.996 --> 05:17.976 align:middle
decision or plan, and no
one knows with any certainty

05:17.976 --> 05:22.186 align:middle
where the economy will be
a year or more from now.

05:22.876 --> 05:25.756 align:middle
Reducing the size of our
balance sheet will also play an

05:25.756 --> 05:29.116 align:middle
important role in firming the
stance of monetary policy.

05:30.266 --> 05:31.856 align:middle
At our meeting that
wrapped up today,

05:32.116 --> 05:34.126 align:middle
the Committee made
good progress on a plan

05:34.126 --> 05:37.256 align:middle
for reducing our securities
holdings, and we expect

05:37.256 --> 05:38.266 align:middle
to announce the beginning

05:38.266 --> 05:40.576 align:middle
of balance sheet reduction
at a coming meeting.

05:41.896 --> 05:43.546 align:middle
In making decisions
about interest rates

05:43.546 --> 05:45.736 align:middle
and the balance sheet,
we will be mindful

05:45.736 --> 05:48.156 align:middle
of the broader context in
markets and in the economy,

05:48.896 --> 05:51.276 align:middle
and we will use our tools
to support financial

05:51.276 --> 05:52.656 align:middle
and macroeconomic stability.

05:54.786 --> 05:57.946 align:middle
As we noted in our policy
statement, the implications

05:57.946 --> 05:59.706 align:middle
of Russia's invasion of Ukraine

05:59.706 --> 06:01.706 align:middle
for the U.S. economy
are highly uncertain.

06:02.846 --> 06:05.636 align:middle
In addition to the direct
effects from higher global oil

06:05.636 --> 06:07.626 align:middle
and commodity prices,
the invasion

06:07.626 --> 06:10.796 align:middle
and related events may restrain
economic activity abroad

06:11.236 --> 06:14.276 align:middle
and further disrupt supply
chains-which would create

06:14.276 --> 06:15.996 align:middle
spillovers to the U.S. economy

06:15.996 --> 06:17.586 align:middle
through trade and
other channels.

06:18.586 --> 06:20.956 align:middle
The volatility in financial
markets, particularly

06:20.956 --> 06:23.976 align:middle
if sustained, could also act
to tighten credit conditions

06:23.976 --> 06:25.186 align:middle
and affect the real economy.

06:27.036 --> 06:28.766 align:middle
Making appropriate
monetary policy

06:28.766 --> 06:30.896 align:middle
in this environment
requires a recognition

06:31.416 --> 06:34.426 align:middle
that the economy often
evolves in unexpected ways.

06:35.216 --> 06:37.746 align:middle
We will need to be nimble in
responding to incoming data

06:37.866 --> 06:38.966 align:middle
and the evolving outlook.

06:39.946 --> 06:42.346 align:middle
And we will strive to
avoid adding uncertainty

06:42.346 --> 06:45.186 align:middle
to what is already an
extraordinarily challenging

06:45.186 --> 06:46.156 align:middle
and uncertain moment.

06:47.566 --> 06:48.956 align:middle
We are attentive to the risks

06:49.026 --> 06:51.556 align:middle
of potential further
upward pressure on inflation

06:51.556 --> 06:53.046 align:middle
and inflation expectations.

06:54.166 --> 06:57.036 align:middle
The Committee is determined
to take the measures necessary

06:57.036 --> 06:58.336 align:middle
to restore price stability.

06:59.586 --> 07:02.456 align:middle
The American economy is very
strong and well positioned

07:02.456 --> 07:04.066 align:middle
to handle tighter
monetary policy.

07:05.526 --> 07:09.276 align:middle
To conclude, we understand that
our actions affect communities,

07:09.326 --> 07:11.416 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

07:12.366 --> 07:14.806 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

07:15.436 --> 07:17.166 align:middle
We at the Fed will
do everything we can

07:17.206 --> 07:18.866 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

07:18.866 --> 07:20.036 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

07:20.976 --> 07:21.316 align:middle
Thank you.

07:21.316 --> 07:22.566 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

07:24.776 --> 07:25.126 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

07:25.126 --> 07:25.376 align:middle
Thank you.

07:25.376 --> 07:26.936 align:middle
Let's go to Jeanna at
the New York Times.

07:26.936 --> 07:27.486 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

07:28.946 --> 07:29.636 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

07:29.636 --> 07:31.876 align:middle
Thank you so much for
taking our questions.

07:31.876 --> 07:35.006 align:middle
I wonder if you could detail
your thinking a little bit

07:35.006 --> 07:37.736 align:middle
about how you're
considering the, you know,

07:37.736 --> 07:40.826 align:middle
the risks of going too fast and
potentially tipping the economy

07:40.826 --> 07:42.736 align:middle
into recession and how
you're weighing those risks

07:42.736 --> 07:46.136 align:middle
against the possibility of going
too slowly, allowing inflation

07:46.136 --> 07:48.476 align:middle
to become embedded, and kind
of getting "behind the curve."

07:50.036 --> 07:50.816 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

07:51.116 --> 07:55.106 align:middle
So I guess I would start
by saying that, in my view,

07:55.106 --> 07:56.526 align:middle
the probability of a recession

07:56.526 --> 07:58.676 align:middle
within the next year is
not particularly elevated.

07:59.236 --> 08:00.266 align:middle
And why do I say that?

08:00.726 --> 08:03.016 align:middle
Aggregate demand is
currently strong,

08:03.016 --> 08:05.396 align:middle
and most forecasters
expect it to remain so.

08:05.446 --> 08:06.626 align:middle
If you look at the labor market,

08:06.786 --> 08:07.766 align:middle
[you find that it
is] also very strong.

08:08.146 --> 08:09.016 align:middle
Conditions are tight,

08:09.016 --> 08:12.296 align:middle
and payroll job growth is
continuing at very high levels.

08:12.716 --> 08:14.826 align:middle
Household and business
balance sheets are strong.

08:15.506 --> 08:19.086 align:middle
And so all signs are that this
is a strong economy and, indeed,

08:19.466 --> 08:24.556 align:middle
one that will be able to
flourish-not to say withstand,

08:24.556 --> 08:28.146 align:middle
but certainly flourish-as
well in the face

08:28.236 --> 08:30.206 align:middle
of less accommodative
monetary policy.

08:30.346 --> 08:33.546 align:middle
So I guess that's how I would
say I'm looking at that.

08:33.546 --> 08:35.196 align:middle
Of course, the objective is

08:35.196 --> 08:38.916 align:middle
to achieve price stability
while also sustaining a strong

08:38.916 --> 08:39.586 align:middle
labor market.

08:39.586 --> 08:41.136 align:middle
And that is our overall
objective.

08:41.136 --> 08:44.356 align:middle
But we do feel the economy is
very strong and well positioned

08:44.356 --> 08:46.486 align:middle
to withstand tighter
monetary policy.

08:46.526 --> 08:47.346 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

08:49.926 --> 08:50.306 align:middle
Thank you.

08:50.306 --> 08:51.746 align:middle
Let's go to Howard at Reuters.

08:53.546 --> 08:54.456 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

08:54.456 --> 08:55.366 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

08:55.406 --> 09:00.056 align:middle
I was wondering, in the SEPs,
you have quite a markdown to GDP

09:00.056 --> 09:01.576 align:middle
from 4 to 2.8 percent.

09:01.576 --> 09:04.636 align:middle
I'm wondering, how much of
that do you feel is the result

09:04.636 --> 09:07.316 align:middle
of the Fed's
stiffer-than-expected

09:07.316 --> 09:08.276 align:middle
action here?

09:08.276 --> 09:10.726 align:middle
You talk about monetary
policy operating with a lag,

09:10.726 --> 09:13.366 align:middle
but is this going to bite
quicker than expected?

09:14.546 --> 09:15.206 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:15.206 --> 09:17.346 align:middle
I don't think that's a
big piece of it, actually.

09:17.346 --> 09:23.676 align:middle
I think some of that is just an
early assessment of the effects

09:23.676 --> 09:26.876 align:middle
of spillovers from the
war in Eastern Europe,

09:27.506 --> 09:29.576 align:middle
which will hit our economy
through a number of channels.

09:29.576 --> 09:32.666 align:middle
Highly uncertain, but,
you know, you're looking

09:32.666 --> 09:35.106 align:middle
at higher oil prices,
higher commodity prices.

09:35.106 --> 09:39.426 align:middle
It'll be-we think that will
weigh on GDP to some extent.

09:39.656 --> 09:42.956 align:middle
So that's part of what moved
those assessments down.

09:42.956 --> 09:45.696 align:middle
I don't think-I mean, I think,
generally, monetary policy works

09:45.696 --> 09:49.146 align:middle
with a lag, so some of that
would also be in there.

09:49.146 --> 09:52.646 align:middle
But remember, as well, that
2.8 percent is still very

09:52.646 --> 09:53.356 align:middle
strong growth.

09:53.356 --> 09:56.016 align:middle
If we think that the
potential growth rate

09:56.016 --> 09:57.616 align:middle
of the economy is
somewhere between,

09:57.876 --> 09:59.736 align:middle
somewhere around 1.75 percent,

09:59.766 --> 10:02.406 align:middle
2.8 percent is strong
economic growth.

10:02.406 --> 10:04.656 align:middle
It would have been one of
the strongest years, in fact,

10:05.006 --> 10:06.076 align:middle
of the last expansion.

10:06.496 --> 10:09.246 align:middle
So, while it's lower than
last year's 5.8 percent,

10:09.246 --> 10:10.566 align:middle
it's still quite strong growth.

10:10.966 --> 10:13.466 align:middle
So I would say it's
quite a strong forecast.

10:14.496 --> 10:15.076 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

10:15.076 --> 10:20.366 align:middle
So, in that context, what
would trigger you to go faster

10:20.366 --> 10:21.586 align:middle
or slower on the rate hikes?

10:21.586 --> 10:24.456 align:middle
"Ongoing increases" doesn't
really tell us whether this is

10:24.456 --> 10:25.856 align:middle
going to come in bigger chunks

10:25.856 --> 10:28.136 align:middle
or evenly paced through
the year.

10:28.476 --> 10:29.026 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:29.026 --> 10:31.496 align:middle
So, you know, the way we're
thinking about this is

10:31.496 --> 10:33.046 align:middle
that every meeting
is a live meeting.

10:33.806 --> 10:36.596 align:middle
And we're going to be looking
at evolving conditions.

10:37.246 --> 10:40.416 align:middle
And if we do conclude that
it would be appropriate

10:40.476 --> 10:45.376 align:middle
to move more quickly to remove
accommodation, then we'll do so.

10:45.776 --> 10:47.986 align:middle
I can't be perfectly
specific about it,

10:48.066 --> 10:49.836 align:middle
but that's certainly
a possibility

10:49.836 --> 10:51.096 align:middle
as we go through the year.

10:51.096 --> 10:52.526 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

10:52.526 --> 10:52.956 align:middle
Thank you.

10:52.956 --> 10:53.626 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

10:53.626 --> 10:54.196 align:middle
Thank you.

10:54.196 --> 10:56.096 align:middle
Let's go to Rachel at
the Washington Post.

10:56.096 --> 10:56.396 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

10:58.306 --> 10:58.856 align:middle
Thank you, Michelle.

10:58.856 --> 11:01.026 align:middle
And thank you, Chair Powell,
for taking our questions.

11:01.286 --> 11:04.716 align:middle
I'm curious if you can be
specific on when you expect

11:04.716 --> 11:07.026 align:middle
to see inflation will
start to come down,

11:07.026 --> 11:09.876 align:middle
especially with the
combination of rates going up,

11:09.876 --> 11:12.786 align:middle
fiscal aid dissipating
from the economy,

11:12.786 --> 11:14.496 align:middle
and supply chains
getting better?

11:14.496 --> 11:17.556 align:middle
And if you don't start to see
that, how will you signal it?

11:17.556 --> 11:19.066 align:middle
What will you be looking for?

11:19.066 --> 11:21.316 align:middle
And what will you be looking
for over the course of the year?

11:21.496 --> 11:21.876 align:middle
Thank you.

11:22.356 --> 11:22.576 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:22.636 --> 11:27.556 align:middle
So I guess I would say that
at the-before the invasion

11:27.556 --> 11:30.376 align:middle
of Ukraine by Russia-so
let's go back to that.

11:30.376 --> 11:32.616 align:middle
I would have said that
the expectation was

11:32.616 --> 11:36.276 align:middle
that inflation would peak
sometime in the first quarter,

11:36.276 --> 11:39.286 align:middle
maybe the end of the first
quarter of this year,

11:39.286 --> 11:42.236 align:middle
and then maybe stay at that
level or a little bit lower

11:42.236 --> 11:43.986 align:middle
and then start to come down
in the back half of the year.

11:43.986 --> 11:45.686 align:middle
So now we're, you know,

11:45.686 --> 11:50.416 align:middle
we're getting-we're going-we're
already seeing a little bit

11:50.416 --> 11:53.776 align:middle
of short-term upward
pressure in inflation due

11:53.776 --> 11:57.936 align:middle
to higher oil prices, natural
gas [prices] a little bit

11:57.936 --> 12:00.746 align:middle
but not so much for us since we
have our own natural gas supply,

12:01.166 --> 12:02.366 align:middle
other commodities prices.

12:02.826 --> 12:07.456 align:middle
The other thing is that you're
seeing supply chain issues

12:07.456 --> 12:12.386 align:middle
around shipping and around,
you know, lots of countries

12:12.386 --> 12:17.256 align:middle
and companies and people not
wanting to touch Russian goods.

12:17.256 --> 12:20.306 align:middle
So that's going to mean
more tangled supply chains.

12:20.576 --> 12:21.726 align:middle
So that could actually push

12:21.726 --> 12:24.436 align:middle
out [in time] the
relief we were expecting

12:24.436 --> 12:25.886 align:middle
on supply chains generally.

12:26.486 --> 12:29.936 align:middle
So I guess I would say that
the expectation is still

12:30.476 --> 12:31.756 align:middle
that inflation will
begin to come

12:31.756 --> 12:33.106 align:middle
down in the second
half of the year.

12:33.106 --> 12:37.186 align:middle
But if you look at where I
read the SEP headline median,

12:37.186 --> 12:39.716 align:middle
we still expect inflation
to be high this year,

12:40.556 --> 12:44.106 align:middle
lower than last year, and then
we expect-though, particularly

12:44.406 --> 12:47.076 align:middle
with the effects of the war,
but also the data we've seen

12:47.076 --> 12:49.686 align:middle
so far this year-we expect
inflation to remain high

12:50.246 --> 12:52.696 align:middle
through the middle of the
year, begin to come down,

12:52.696 --> 12:54.246 align:middle
and then come down
more sharply next year.

12:54.336 --> 12:55.586 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

12:59.326 --> 13:01.096 align:middle
Let's go to Nick at the
Wall Street Journal.

13:01.096 --> 13:03.696 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

13:03.696 --> 13:05.136 align:middle
Nick Timiraos of the
Wall Street Journal.

13:05.386 --> 13:07.656 align:middle
Chair Powell, over
the last six months,

13:07.656 --> 13:10.716 align:middle
the Fed has shifted its
policy stance quite a bit.

13:10.716 --> 13:13.366 align:middle
Six months ago, you were
still buying assets.

13:13.366 --> 13:15.996 align:middle
Most officials weren't
projecting any interest rate

13:15.996 --> 13:17.076 align:middle
increases this year.

13:17.546 --> 13:21.556 align:middle
And yet, despite the shift
over the last six months,

13:21.556 --> 13:25.146 align:middle
real rates are as negative
today as they were then.

13:25.276 --> 13:26.826 align:middle
So how concerned are you

13:27.186 --> 13:32.456 align:middle
that further inflation surprises
will offset the effects

13:32.456 --> 13:35.866 align:middle
of recent policy firming by
leading real rates to stay

13:35.866 --> 13:40.016 align:middle
at levels that do not
actually provide much restraint

13:40.396 --> 13:41.566 align:middle
to the economy?

13:41.636 --> 13:43.106 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

13:43.106 --> 13:48.456 align:middle
So that's one of many ways
of capturing the situation,

13:48.456 --> 13:51.046 align:middle
which is that we-the Committee
really does understand

13:51.046 --> 13:54.076 align:middle
that the time for rate increases

13:54.076 --> 13:57.136 align:middle
and for shrinking the
balance sheet has come.

13:57.226 --> 13:59.556 align:middle
And I would just
say, I would go back

14:00.376 --> 14:03.216 align:middle
to "the economy is very
strong," as I mentioned.

14:03.776 --> 14:05.906 align:middle
Tremendous momentum
in the labor market.

14:05.906 --> 14:07.286 align:middle
We expect growth to continue.

14:07.976 --> 14:10.926 align:middle
As I-it's clearly time
to raise interest rates

14:10.926 --> 14:12.686 align:middle
and begin the balance
sheet shrinkage.

14:12.986 --> 14:17.206 align:middle
And I just wanted to say that
as I looked around the table

14:17.206 --> 14:19.996 align:middle
at today's meeting, I saw a
Committee that's acutely aware

14:20.206 --> 14:22.406 align:middle
of the need to return the
economy to price stability

14:23.356 --> 14:26.026 align:middle
and [is] determined to use
our tools to do exactly that.

14:26.766 --> 14:28.746 align:middle
You couched it in
terms of real rates.

14:28.746 --> 14:31.606 align:middle
I would say, if you
look at the SEP,

14:31.606 --> 14:35.466 align:middle
you've got people getting close
to or even above, in many cases,

14:35.946 --> 14:38.206 align:middle
their estimate of the
longer-run neutral rate.

14:39.086 --> 14:41.356 align:middle
So I understand that
doesn't do it for real rates.

14:41.356 --> 14:43.586 align:middle
But if you go out a year
or two, many people are,

14:43.586 --> 14:47.816 align:middle
in their forecasts,
are having tight policy

14:47.816 --> 14:49.816 align:middle
from a real interest
rate standpoint.

14:49.816 --> 14:52.186 align:middle
So that's something
that we're focused on.

14:52.296 --> 14:55.856 align:middle
Of course, it's a highly
uncertain environment.

14:55.856 --> 14:59.626 align:middle
And, you know, we don't
know what's going to happen.

14:59.626 --> 15:02.326 align:middle
But we do know that we're
going to deploy our tools

15:03.546 --> 15:05.916 align:middle
to achieve our goals-and

15:05.916 --> 15:07.426 align:middle
that includes the
price-stability goal.

15:07.426 --> 15:08.336 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

15:10.166 --> 15:13.016 align:middle
Let's go to Victoria
at Politico.

15:16.046 --> 15:16.606 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

15:16.606 --> 15:17.216 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

15:17.216 --> 15:20.066 align:middle
So, looking at the Summary
of Economic Projections,

15:20.066 --> 15:23.076 align:middle
you all have inflation
coming down over the course

15:23.076 --> 15:25.606 align:middle
of the year to 4.3 percent.

15:25.606 --> 15:28.616 align:middle
And then you also have rates
going up to what appears

15:28.616 --> 15:32.656 align:middle
to still be below roughly
what would be estimated

15:32.656 --> 15:34.416 align:middle
to be the neutral rate,
although I know that's a little

15:34.416 --> 15:34.916 align:middle
bit uncertain.

15:34.916 --> 15:37.526 align:middle
So I was just wondering,
how much of inflation coming

15:37.526 --> 15:40.086 align:middle
down do you see as
actually being as a result

15:40.486 --> 15:43.456 align:middle
of the Fed itself raising rates?

15:43.456 --> 15:45.376 align:middle
And then also, if
I could just ask,

15:45.666 --> 15:48.856 align:middle
given that Sarah Bloom Raskin
withdrew her nomination,

15:49.296 --> 15:51.796 align:middle
what do you expect to do with
the regulatory portfolio?

15:51.796 --> 15:55.226 align:middle
Do you expect to assign a
Governor in charge of that?

15:55.856 --> 15:56.266 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:57.276 --> 15:57.806 align:middle
Okay, sorry.

15:58.036 --> 16:00.266 align:middle
Tell me again your
first question.

16:00.476 --> 16:01.036 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

16:01.036 --> 16:03.936 align:middle
My first question is, how much
do you expect inflation to come

16:03.936 --> 16:05.946 align:middle
down as a direct result
of the Fed's actions?

16:06.226 --> 16:07.216 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:07.806 --> 16:11.276 align:middle
Okay. So part of
inflation coming

16:11.276 --> 16:15.266 align:middle
down at the very beginning is
clearly to do with factors other

16:15.266 --> 16:17.496 align:middle
than our policy, and those
would include, potentially,

16:17.496 --> 16:19.236 align:middle
the supply chain is
getting a little bit better,

16:19.236 --> 16:20.346 align:middle
certainly base effects.

16:20.346 --> 16:22.576 align:middle
You're lapping-as you
know, when you look

16:22.576 --> 16:24.216 align:middle
at a 12-month trailing window,

16:24.216 --> 16:27.116 align:middle
you're lapping very high
inflation in March, April,

16:27.116 --> 16:28.296 align:middle
May, June of last year.

16:28.296 --> 16:30.666 align:middle
So there should be
some effects from that

16:31.456 --> 16:32.766 align:middle
in the 12-month picture.

16:32.796 --> 16:33.926 align:middle
Really what we're
looking for, though,

16:33.926 --> 16:36.066 align:middle
is month-by-month
inflation coming down.

16:36.066 --> 16:39.846 align:middle
And so it's really, it's all the
things we've been talking about,

16:40.106 --> 16:42.446 align:middle
you know, that really
haven't helped much,

16:42.446 --> 16:45.456 align:middle
including the shift away from
goods and back to services,

16:45.456 --> 16:47.516 align:middle
including supply
chains getting better,

16:47.516 --> 16:50.376 align:middle
including labor force
participation-all those things

16:50.376 --> 16:52.446 align:middle
that have been sticky
and not happening.

16:52.976 --> 16:54.786 align:middle
But a big part of it is, though,

16:54.786 --> 16:56.756 align:middle
is the base effects
I mentioned as well.

16:57.236 --> 16:59.546 align:middle
You know, I think monetary
policy starts to bite

17:00.056 --> 17:04.186 align:middle
on inflation, and on growth,
with a lag, of course.

17:04.596 --> 17:07.996 align:middle
And so you would see
that more in '23 and '24.

17:08.126 --> 17:10.466 align:middle
But also, remember
we started talking

17:10.466 --> 17:11.866 align:middle
about rate increases last year.

17:11.866 --> 17:15.126 align:middle
For some months now, financial
conditions have already

17:15.126 --> 17:17.376 align:middle
incorporated a significant
number of rate increases.

17:17.376 --> 17:19.316 align:middle
So it doesn't start today.

17:19.316 --> 17:22.866 align:middle
The effect, the moves
are already priced

17:22.866 --> 17:24.816 align:middle
into the market for
a few months now.

17:24.816 --> 17:28.576 align:middle
So the clock is running
on that, and I think some

17:28.576 --> 17:31.026 align:middle
of that will be seen in the
second half of the year as well.

17:31.026 --> 17:37.066 align:middle
So on the regulatory portfolio,
I would just say this.

17:37.066 --> 17:39.696 align:middle
You know, we have an obligation
to carry out under the law

17:40.576 --> 17:43.366 align:middle
in supervision and regulation,
and we're doing that.

17:43.566 --> 17:44.516 align:middle
That's what we're doing.

17:45.006 --> 17:47.046 align:middle
The committee is not active.

17:47.236 --> 17:49.806 align:middle
So what's happening is, things
are coming to the full Board,

17:50.076 --> 17:50.846 align:middle
and we're voting on them.

17:50.846 --> 17:52.016 align:middle
We're getting our business done.

17:52.016 --> 17:54.456 align:middle
You know, we got the
stress tests done.

17:54.576 --> 17:56.986 align:middle
We've looked at, you know,
a number of proposals

17:56.986 --> 17:58.386 align:middle
for mergers and things
like that.

17:58.686 --> 18:00.726 align:middle
So we're working ahead.

18:00.726 --> 18:02.146 align:middle
Of course, we don't
have a Vice Chair

18:02.146 --> 18:03.426 align:middle
for Supervision,
as you mentioned.

18:04.046 --> 18:07.546 align:middle
But we're making do with
the situation we have.

18:07.546 --> 18:09.706 align:middle
And a good number of
things have come straight

18:09.706 --> 18:10.636 align:middle
to the Board for approval.

18:12.426 --> 18:12.986 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

18:14.416 --> 18:16.416 align:middle
Let's go to Neil Irwin at Axios.

18:23.446 --> 18:24.756 align:middle
[Pause] Let's go to Neil Irwin.

18:24.756 --> 18:26.206 align:middle
NEIL IRWIN.

18:26.206 --> 18:26.986 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

18:26.986 --> 18:28.136 align:middle
It's Neil Irwin at Axios.

18:28.136 --> 18:29.596 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

18:29.806 --> 18:33.796 align:middle
In the statement of economic
projections, we see a forecast

18:33.796 --> 18:35.796 align:middle
of median 1.9 percent
fed funds rate at the end

18:35.796 --> 18:37.856 align:middle
of the year, 2.8
end of next year.

18:38.076 --> 18:40.456 align:middle
Wondered whether that aligns
with your own expectations,

18:40.456 --> 18:42.246 align:middle
in particular on that point

18:42.316 --> 18:44.666 align:middle
of overshooting the
long-term neutral rate.

18:44.906 --> 18:47.376 align:middle
And, also, if you can
tell us anything about how

18:47.376 --> 18:49.476 align:middle
that might be paced:
front-loaded, back-loaded.

18:49.656 --> 18:51.546 align:middle
How high is the bar for
doing 50 at one meeting?

18:53.126 --> 18:53.866 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:53.986 --> 18:55.846 align:middle
So I don't-Neil,
I've never talked

18:56.056 --> 18:58.576 align:middle
about my own SEP projection.

18:58.576 --> 19:00.076 align:middle
It's in there, but I, you know,

19:00.076 --> 19:02.386 align:middle
I think Fed Chairs have
generally not done that,

19:02.386 --> 19:04.636 align:middle
because we just haven't done it.

19:04.636 --> 19:05.606 align:middle
It's because we're, you know,

19:05.696 --> 19:08.996 align:middle
we have to put together the
consensus on the Committee

19:08.996 --> 19:10.356 align:middle
and present that consensus.

19:10.356 --> 19:13.576 align:middle
So I wouldn't talk
about my individual one.

19:13.576 --> 19:17.106 align:middle
And in terms of the pacing of
it, I would just point out that

19:17.956 --> 19:20.316 align:middle
that is-there's seven
remaining meetings this year.

19:20.556 --> 19:22.996 align:middle
This isn't something we
discuss or debate or agree on.

19:22.996 --> 19:24.106 align:middle
But there's seven
remaining meetings,

19:24.106 --> 19:25.186 align:middle
and there's seven rate hikes.

19:25.806 --> 19:29.486 align:middle
I would add there's also the
shrinkage of the balance sheet,

19:29.486 --> 19:32.046 align:middle
which, you people do
the math different ways,

19:32.046 --> 19:34.846 align:middle
but that might be the equivalent
of another rate increase just

19:34.846 --> 19:38.686 align:middle
from the runoff of
the balance sheet.

19:39.266 --> 19:41.756 align:middle
So I don't-but I don't know.

19:41.756 --> 19:44.536 align:middle
We haven't made any
decisions on front-end loading

19:44.536 --> 19:46.756 align:middle
or going steadily
through the year.

19:47.076 --> 19:51.706 align:middle
And, as I mentioned, you
know, if you look at the SEP,

19:51.706 --> 19:54.996 align:middle
a good number of participants
do see more than seven

19:54.996 --> 19:56.096 align:middle
or eight increases this year.

19:56.656 --> 19:59.936 align:middle
And I can't give you,
I'm not going to try

19:59.936 --> 20:01.516 align:middle
to give you a really
specific test

20:01.516 --> 20:02.596 align:middle
for what it would
take to do that.

20:02.626 --> 20:05.016 align:middle
But I will say this,
that we'll be looking

20:05.016 --> 20:07.066 align:middle
at the data as they come in.

20:07.466 --> 20:10.486 align:middle
We'll be looking to see
whether the data show expected

20:10.486 --> 20:11.956 align:middle
improvement on inflation.

20:11.956 --> 20:13.896 align:middle
We'll be looking at
the inflation outlook

20:14.486 --> 20:15.576 align:middle
and making a judgement.

20:15.646 --> 20:18.306 align:middle
And we'll be going-each
meeting is a live meeting.

20:18.306 --> 20:20.386 align:middle
And if we conclude that
it would be appropriate

20:20.436 --> 20:24.696 align:middle
to raise interest rates more
quickly, then we'll do so.

20:25.356 --> 20:25.876 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

21:03.336 --> 21:03.896 align:middle
[Long pause] One second.

21:12.106 --> 21:13.416 align:middle
Okay. We're going to
try one more time.

21:14.246 --> 21:41.876 align:middle
Colby Smith at the FT.

21:41.876 --> 21:42.076 align:middle
[Long pause] Okay.

21:42.076 --> 21:43.126 align:middle
We'll come back to Colby.

21:43.126 --> 21:44.416 align:middle
Let's go to Edward Lawrence.

21:44.416 --> 21:45.076 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

21:48.896 --> 21:49.986 align:middle
Yes. Thank you, Chair Powell.

21:49.986 --> 21:51.316 align:middle
Thank you, Michelle,
for the question.

21:51.316 --> 21:52.606 align:middle
I have a more basic question.

21:52.606 --> 21:55.706 align:middle
The last time the CPI
inflation-I know you look

21:55.706 --> 21:59.476 align:middle
at PCE, but CPI inflation-was as
high as it is was July of 1981,

21:59.476 --> 22:02.546 align:middle
when the effective federal
funds rate was 19.2 percent.

22:02.756 --> 22:06.156 align:middle
But given the current data,
how far behind the curve

22:06.226 --> 22:08.216 align:middle
of inflation do you believe
the Federal Reserve is,

22:08.216 --> 22:12.766 align:middle
in your mind?

22:12.766 --> 22:13.226 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:13.426 --> 22:17.956 align:middle
So I just would say
a couple things.

22:17.956 --> 22:23.866 align:middle
We have the tools that we need,
and we're going to use them.

22:24.296 --> 22:26.586 align:middle
And, as you can see, we
have a plan over the course

22:26.586 --> 22:30.266 align:middle
of this year to raise interest
rates steadily and also

22:30.266 --> 22:31.316 align:middle
to run off the balance sheet.

22:32.216 --> 22:34.396 align:middle
We will take the
necessary steps to ensure

22:34.396 --> 22:36.356 align:middle
that high inflation does
not become entrenched,

22:36.436 --> 22:38.206 align:middle
while also supporting
a strong labor market.

22:38.936 --> 22:40.246 align:middle
And, as I mentioned,
if we conclude

22:40.246 --> 22:43.146 align:middle
that it would be appropriate to
move more quickly, we'll do so.

22:44.216 --> 22:47.506 align:middle
I'll leave it to others to make
the judgment you asked for.

22:48.656 --> 22:49.206 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

22:49.206 --> 22:51.416 align:middle
And then just as a follow
on that, I wanted to get you

22:51.416 --> 22:52.576 align:middle
on the record on this.

22:52.576 --> 22:55.396 align:middle
What impact has there been
on your job, given the fact

22:55.396 --> 22:55.696 align:middle
that [indaudible]- CHAIR POWELL.

23:00.886 --> 23:02.246 align:middle
We lost you in the
middle of the question.

23:05.846 --> 23:07.206 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

23:08.176 --> 23:09.806 align:middle
Edward, can you repeat
that question?

23:14.646 --> 23:15.776 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

23:15.776 --> 23:18.136 align:middle
-the impact that-given
you're not actually confirmed

23:18.426 --> 23:20.516 align:middle
and Governor Brainard is
not actually confirmed,

23:20.516 --> 23:22.396 align:middle
has there been any
impact on your job

23:22.716 --> 23:25.006 align:middle
or the Fed's ability
to handle inflation?

23:25.466 --> 23:25.676 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:25.806 --> 23:26.546 align:middle
None whatsoever.

23:26.776 --> 23:27.836 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

23:32.236 --> 23:34.026 align:middle
Okay, let's go to
Colby Smith at the FT.

23:34.026 --> 23:35.536 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

23:35.536 --> 23:36.446 align:middle
Thank you, Michelle.

23:36.676 --> 23:38.456 align:middle
Chair Powell, how
concerned is the Committee

23:38.456 --> 23:40.826 align:middle
about the notable pickup
in services inflation,

23:41.006 --> 23:43.106 align:middle
which is perhaps less
likely to self-correct?

23:43.556 --> 23:46.076 align:middle
And to what extent does it alter
both the Committee's confidence

23:46.076 --> 23:49.946 align:middle
that long-term inflation
expectations will not de-anchor

23:49.946 --> 23:52.156 align:middle
in the coming months as
well as the balance of risks

23:52.156 --> 23:54.676 align:middle
that the Fed may need to
raise interest rates further

23:54.676 --> 23:57.116 align:middle
above neutral than
indicated in the dot plot?

23:57.346 --> 23:57.646 align:middle
Thank you.

23:57.646 --> 23:57.856 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:57.856 --> 23:58.706 align:middle
Thank you.

23:59.386 --> 24:03.066 align:middle
So, of course, that's something
we're watching report by report.

24:03.066 --> 24:05.256 align:middle
And we're certainly, we
noticed it in the last meeting.

24:05.256 --> 24:07.616 align:middle
And it's part of
the overall picture.

24:08.146 --> 24:11.356 align:middle
We have expected services
inflation to move back up to

24:11.356 --> 24:13.626 align:middle
where it was, and that's
part of what's happening is,

24:14.106 --> 24:18.136 align:middle
in the case of some services,
prices are still getting back

24:18.136 --> 24:20.496 align:middle
up to where they were
before the crisis.

24:20.496 --> 24:22.836 align:middle
In other cases, it's
pretty clear

24:22.836 --> 24:25.796 align:middle
that inflation has spread
more broadly across services.

24:25.796 --> 24:27.326 align:middle
And, yes, that is concerning.

24:27.866 --> 24:30.476 align:middle
In the meanwhile, we see some
progress on the good side.

24:30.476 --> 24:33.436 align:middle
But, really, in this latest
report, it was confined

24:33.436 --> 24:38.966 align:middle
to vehicles, which is,
admittedly, a large category.

24:39.326 --> 24:41.816 align:middle
So, you know, as I mentioned,

24:42.046 --> 24:49.566 align:middle
the Committee acutely feels its
obligation to move to make sure

24:49.566 --> 24:52.336 align:middle
that we restore price
stability and is determined

24:52.336 --> 24:53.576 align:middle
to use its tools to do so.

24:53.576 --> 24:54.436 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

24:56.826 --> 24:57.196 align:middle
Thank you.

24:57.196 --> 24:59.026 align:middle
Let's go to Steve
Liesman at CNBC.

24:59.026 --> 24:59.976 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

25:02.116 --> 25:02.756 align:middle
Thank you.

25:02.866 --> 25:06.546 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, I wonder if you
can help me understand the kind

25:06.546 --> 25:10.516 align:middle
of logic, if there is
one, in the SEP here.

25:10.516 --> 25:14.936 align:middle
As I look at the median
forecast, for example,

25:14.936 --> 25:17.546 align:middle
for unemployment, it runs

25:17.546 --> 25:20.906 align:middle
for the three-year window
below the long-run rate.

25:23.076 --> 25:27.236 align:middle
I look at inflation being above,
over the three-year window,

25:27.626 --> 25:29.786 align:middle
the long-run-or call
it the neutral-rate,

25:29.786 --> 25:31.286 align:middle
so the economy still runs hot.

25:31.836 --> 25:35.376 align:middle
And that is all true in a
regime when you run at least

25:35.376 --> 25:38.966 align:middle
for two years the funds rate
above the long-run rate.

25:39.506 --> 25:41.166 align:middle
I guess my question is this:

25:41.476 --> 25:44.406 align:middle
Are you not create-giving
a forecast here

25:44.726 --> 25:47.576 align:middle
that essentially suggests
you will be continuing

25:47.576 --> 25:50.666 align:middle
to run further "behind the
curve"-and never really get

25:50.906 --> 25:52.916 align:middle
in front of inflation, because
the economy will continue

25:52.916 --> 25:53.576 align:middle
to run hot?

25:53.946 --> 25:56.076 align:middle
And, kind of on a related issue:

25:56.076 --> 25:59.746 align:middle
You said earlier [that]
inflation will take longer

25:59.746 --> 26:03.646 align:middle
to return to price stability
than we had originally expected.

26:03.996 --> 26:05.566 align:middle
Isn't that a choice
you're making?

26:05.566 --> 26:09.086 align:middle
And, if so, why are you making
that choice to let inflation run

26:09.086 --> 26:12.386 align:middle
above price stability
longer than you'd like?

26:14.256 --> 26:14.826 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:14.826 --> 26:18.686 align:middle
Right. So our, if you
look, our-first of all,

26:18.686 --> 26:20.166 align:middle
there is no-you're
looking at medians.

26:20.166 --> 26:23.786 align:middle
But understand that there's no,
it's not something we voted on.

26:23.786 --> 26:24.546 align:middle
It's not a plan.

26:25.036 --> 26:29.526 align:middle
But if you look, people do
have their in-by the end

26:29.526 --> 26:33.356 align:middle
of this year, broadly,
people are at or close to or,

26:33.356 --> 26:35.576 align:middle
in some cases, above
their estimates

26:35.576 --> 26:37.096 align:middle
of longer-run neutral
interest rate.

26:37.286 --> 26:41.596 align:middle
Okay. So that should
stop pushing-that should,

26:41.756 --> 26:44.246 align:middle
in other words, that should
be a removal of accommodation

26:44.246 --> 26:45.626 align:middle
for monetary policy, basically.

26:45.876 --> 26:48.066 align:middle
At the same time, we will
have done significant balance

26:48.066 --> 26:48.706 align:middle
sheet runoff.

26:49.086 --> 26:50.996 align:middle
And you can think
of that as further.

26:51.566 --> 26:54.476 align:middle
In the next year, and just
looking at the median,

26:54.936 --> 26:59.246 align:middle
you're now above the, you know,
above the, what people estimate

26:59.246 --> 27:02.056 align:middle
to be the longer-run median.

27:02.056 --> 27:05.716 align:middle
And also, in many
people's forecasts,

27:05.716 --> 27:08.696 align:middle
that actually amounts to,
you know, tight policy under,

27:08.696 --> 27:09.896 align:middle
in real rates as well.

27:10.416 --> 27:13.316 align:middle
So why does unemployment
remain at 3.5 percent?

27:13.316 --> 27:16.496 align:middle
You know, it-I mean,
a couple points.

27:16.496 --> 27:20.986 align:middle
One is the connection between-in
the economy we had before the

27:20.986 --> 27:23.276 align:middle
pandemic, the connection
between inflation and the level

27:23.276 --> 27:25.516 align:middle
of the unemployment
rate was not very tight.

27:26.116 --> 27:29.196 align:middle
But this is-clearly, what this
is is an expectation that,

27:29.676 --> 27:32.736 align:middle
really it amounts to that,
the idea that wage increases,

27:32.736 --> 27:35.946 align:middle
which are now running above the
level that would be consistent

27:35.946 --> 27:38.846 align:middle
over the long run with 2 percent
inflation, will move back

27:38.846 --> 27:42.296 align:middle
down to levels which are
still very attractive,

27:42.296 --> 27:44.966 align:middle
full-economy kind
of-full-employment kind

27:44.966 --> 27:47.606 align:middle
of wages, but not to a point

27:47.606 --> 27:51.576 align:middle
where they're pushing
up inflation anymore.

27:52.376 --> 27:53.616 align:middle
And, as I mentioned,
there are a lot

27:53.616 --> 27:56.246 align:middle
of factors causing
inflation to come down.

27:56.936 --> 28:00.116 align:middle
And, you know, the
reality is, there are many,

28:00.116 --> 28:02.676 align:middle
many moving pieces, and we don't
know what will actually happen.

28:03.246 --> 28:05.716 align:middle
But no matter what
happens, this is a Committee

28:05.716 --> 28:08.116 align:middle
that is determined to use
its tools to make sure

28:08.116 --> 28:10.636 align:middle
that higher inflation does
not become entrenched.

28:11.406 --> 28:14.536 align:middle
And so we are determined
on that front,

28:14.536 --> 28:15.986 align:middle
and we'll deal with what comes.

28:16.446 --> 28:19.556 align:middle
This is a modal, or most
likely, expectation.

28:19.556 --> 28:22.476 align:middle
But we'll deal with what comes,
whether it's better or worse.

28:23.036 --> 28:23.786 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

28:27.846 --> 28:28.196 align:middle
Thank you.

28:28.196 --> 28:29.856 align:middle
Let's go to Chris
Rugaber at the AP.

28:29.856 --> 28:30.746 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

28:32.566 --> 28:33.646 align:middle
Hi. Thank you.

28:34.326 --> 28:36.586 align:middle
Well, let me follow
up a bit on that.

28:36.586 --> 28:39.736 align:middle
I mean, there are a lot
of economists skeptical

28:39.736 --> 28:44.376 align:middle
that you can reduce inflation
as much as you've penciled

28:44.376 --> 28:47.476 align:middle
in without raising
the unemployment rate.

28:47.476 --> 28:51.986 align:middle
And I'm wondering, just what
are the mechanisms you see

28:51.986 --> 28:53.366 align:middle
in reducing demand?

28:53.366 --> 28:55.226 align:middle
I mean, outside housing
and autos,

28:55.626 --> 29:00.066 align:middle
how do higher fed [funds] rates
reduce consumer demand unless

29:00.066 --> 29:02.636 align:middle
it's through higher
unemployment?

29:02.726 --> 29:03.176 align:middle
Thank you.

29:04.006 --> 29:04.856 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:04.976 --> 29:08.466 align:middle
Well, if you take a look
at today's labor market,

29:08.696 --> 29:13.736 align:middle
what you have is
1.7-plus job openings

29:13.786 --> 29:15.956 align:middle
for every unemployed person.

29:16.296 --> 29:20.936 align:middle
So that's a very, very
tight labor market-tight

29:21.036 --> 29:22.556 align:middle
to an unhealthy level,
I would say.

29:22.556 --> 29:26.136 align:middle
So, in principle, if
you were-let's say

29:26.136 --> 29:29.246 align:middle
that our tools work about as
you described, and the idea is,

29:29.246 --> 29:32.106 align:middle
we're trying to better
align demand and supply,

29:32.206 --> 29:33.376 align:middle
let's just say in
the labor market.

29:33.376 --> 29:37.776 align:middle
So it would actually, if you
were just moving down the number

29:37.776 --> 29:41.476 align:middle
of job openings so that they
were more like one to one,

29:41.476 --> 29:43.676 align:middle
you would have less
upward pressure on wages.

29:43.986 --> 29:46.896 align:middle
You would have a lot less of a
labor shortage, which is going

29:46.896 --> 29:49.016 align:middle
on pretty much across
the economy.

29:49.016 --> 29:50.386 align:middle
We're hearing from companies

29:50.416 --> 29:52.706 align:middle
that they can't hire
enough people.

29:52.706 --> 29:53.826 align:middle
They're having a
hard time hiring.

29:54.446 --> 29:58.466 align:middle
So that's really the
thinking there, is, you know,

29:58.466 --> 30:02.796 align:middle
these are fairly well-understood
channels, interest sensitive.

30:02.796 --> 30:05.446 align:middle
And, basically, across
the economy,

30:05.446 --> 30:09.546 align:middle
we'd like to slow demand so that
it's better aligned with supply;

30:09.756 --> 30:12.546 align:middle
give supply, at the same
time, time to recover;

30:12.896 --> 30:14.706 align:middle
and get into a better, you know,

30:14.706 --> 30:16.676 align:middle
a better alignment
of supply and demand.

30:16.676 --> 30:18.886 align:middle
And that, over time,
should bring inflation down.

30:19.886 --> 30:21.836 align:middle
And I'll say again,
though, you know,

30:21.836 --> 30:25.036 align:middle
we don't have a perfect
crystal ball about the future,

30:25.786 --> 30:28.086 align:middle
and we're prepared to
use our tools as needed

30:28.086 --> 30:29.776 align:middle
to restore price stability.

30:30.196 --> 30:32.326 align:middle
You know, as I mentioned
in my opening remarks,

30:32.326 --> 30:33.466 align:middle
without price stability,

30:34.126 --> 30:36.986 align:middle
there-you really can't
have a sustained period

30:36.986 --> 30:38.186 align:middle
of maximum employment.

30:38.186 --> 30:41.366 align:middle
It's our-one of our most
fundamental obligations is

30:41.366 --> 30:45.626 align:middle
to maintain and restore, in
this case, price stability.

30:45.916 --> 30:47.226 align:middle
So we're very committed to that.

30:47.946 --> 30:49.366 align:middle
Of course, the plan is

30:49.366 --> 30:53.046 align:middle
to restore price stability
while also sustaining a strong

30:53.046 --> 30:53.596 align:middle
labor market.

30:53.596 --> 30:55.536 align:middle
That is our intention, and
we believe we can do that.

30:55.896 --> 30:57.476 align:middle
But we have to restore
price stability.

30:58.466 --> 30:59.496 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

31:00.836 --> 31:02.766 align:middle
Okay. Let's go to
Scott Horsley at NPR.

31:02.766 --> 31:06.976 align:middle
SCOTT HORSLEY.

31:06.976 --> 31:09.776 align:middle
Thanks. And I apologize
if this covers some

31:09.776 --> 31:12.346 align:middle
of the same ground you just
talked about, Mr. Chair.

31:12.346 --> 31:13.886 align:middle
I think I missed some
of your answer there.

31:13.886 --> 31:17.676 align:middle
But I have a follow-up
question on the labor force.

31:17.896 --> 31:20.626 align:middle
We have seen some gains
in the prime-age workforce

31:20.626 --> 31:21.686 align:middle
in the last few months.

31:21.946 --> 31:25.156 align:middle
I wonder what you anticipate
when it comes to some

31:25.156 --> 31:28.556 align:middle
of the older workers as the
health outlook has changed.

31:28.916 --> 31:32.646 align:middle
Are we going to see more recent
retirees following Tom Brady

31:32.646 --> 31:33.656 align:middle
back into the workforce?

31:33.656 --> 31:37.126 align:middle
And what would that mean
for wages and inflation?

31:37.126 --> 31:38.746 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:38.826 --> 31:39.556 align:middle
It's hard to say.

31:39.556 --> 31:43.936 align:middle
You know, what we saw in
the last cycle was that,

31:43.936 --> 31:46.636 align:middle
over the course of a
long, steady expansion,

31:47.246 --> 31:51.316 align:middle
labor force participation
outperformed expectations.

31:51.986 --> 31:55.226 align:middle
And that was just a, you know,
it was a tight labor market,

31:55.226 --> 31:56.496 align:middle
but it wasn't-it
was nowhere near

31:56.496 --> 31:57.696 align:middle
as tight as this labor market.

31:57.696 --> 32:00.366 align:middle
But it was a tight labor
market, and so people stayed

32:00.366 --> 32:01.506 align:middle
in the labor force longer.

32:01.806 --> 32:03.666 align:middle
It wasn't so much
people coming back

32:03.666 --> 32:05.226 align:middle
in the labor force
after retirement.

32:05.746 --> 32:07.736 align:middle
That's not something
that happens,

32:08.536 --> 32:10.016 align:middle
in the aggregate, very much.

32:10.116 --> 32:12.736 align:middle
But, so that's what
was happening.

32:12.736 --> 32:15.416 align:middle
And, you know, more labor
force participation is

32:15.416 --> 32:16.576 align:middle
tremendously welcome.

32:16.576 --> 32:20.406 align:middle
And, of course, our policy does
not in any way preclude that.

32:20.456 --> 32:22.586 align:middle
This is a situation
where wages have moved

32:22.586 --> 32:26.096 align:middle
up at the highest rate
in a very long time.

32:26.606 --> 32:29.496 align:middle
And people are able to
quit their jobs and move

32:29.496 --> 32:31.596 align:middle
to better-paying jobs
in the same industry

32:31.596 --> 32:32.366 align:middle
or different industries.

32:32.366 --> 32:35.046 align:middle
So it's a really attractive
labor market for people.

32:35.546 --> 32:39.126 align:middle
And once, you know, as we get
past COVID well and truly,

32:39.596 --> 32:41.446 align:middle
it becomes an even
more attractive one.

32:41.446 --> 32:43.986 align:middle
So we hope that that will
lead to more labor supply.

32:44.406 --> 32:45.556 align:middle
That's a good thing
for the country.

32:45.556 --> 32:46.616 align:middle
It's a good thing for people.

32:47.176 --> 32:50.546 align:middle
And it also will, we
think, help relieve some

32:50.706 --> 32:54.596 align:middle
of the wage pressures that do
put inflation more at risk.

32:55.606 --> 32:59.446 align:middle
That last part is, we'll have
to see whether, empirically,

32:59.446 --> 33:00.846 align:middle
it winds up, works out that way.

33:00.846 --> 33:03.936 align:middle
But, in principle, it ought to
help with inflation as well.

33:04.546 --> 33:06.306 align:middle
It's not the only thing
we're looking for,

33:06.306 --> 33:07.076 align:middle
though, from inflation.

33:07.076 --> 33:12.346 align:middle
We're looking for help from a
number of different places and,

33:12.346 --> 33:14.096 align:middle
most importantly,
from our own policy.

33:14.096 --> 33:15.226 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:18.586 --> 33:21.196 align:middle
Okay. Let's go to Rich
Miller at Bloomberg.

33:21.266 --> 33:21.806 align:middle
RICH MILLER.

33:24.536 --> 33:26.336 align:middle
Thank you, Michelle.

33:26.336 --> 33:27.396 align:middle
And thank you, Chair Powell.

33:27.396 --> 33:29.366 align:middle
I'm sorry; I'm having some
communication problems.

33:29.366 --> 33:32.956 align:middle
So I missed some of the stuff
you've said, and my apologies

33:32.956 --> 33:34.346 align:middle
if this has been asked.

33:34.346 --> 33:38.236 align:middle
Since the FOMC met last January,

33:38.236 --> 33:41.016 align:middle
financial conditions
have tightened markedly,

33:41.516 --> 33:45.606 align:middle
equity prices down, Treasury
yield's up, bond spread's risen,

33:45.606 --> 33:47.126 align:middle
yield curve has flattened a lot,

33:47.126 --> 33:50.916 align:middle
and even further just
today, dollar's up.

33:51.236 --> 33:52.416 align:middle
Is that welcome?

33:52.606 --> 33:56.006 align:middle
And would you like to see more
in order to achieve your goals?

33:56.006 --> 33:56.466 align:middle
Thank you.

33:56.526 --> 33:56.736 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:56.736 --> 34:00.106 align:middle
So, as you know, policy works
through financial conditions.

34:00.106 --> 34:03.836 align:middle
That's how it reaches the real
economy-by just the mechanisms

34:03.836 --> 34:04.416 align:middle
you mentioned.

34:05.026 --> 34:09.116 align:middle
And remember that the
financial conditions we had

34:09.796 --> 34:12.396 align:middle
for the last couple of years
were a function not only

34:12.396 --> 34:15.936 align:middle
of very aggressive, and
appropriately so, fiscal policy,

34:15.936 --> 34:19.726 align:middle
but also highly accommodative
monetary policy-the monetary

34:19.726 --> 34:21.546 align:middle
policy settings that
we put in place

34:21.656 --> 34:23.976 align:middle
at the worst parts
of the pandemic.

34:24.326 --> 34:27.216 align:middle
So it is very appropriate
to move away from those.

34:27.216 --> 34:29.246 align:middle
And, yes, that will
lead to some tightening

34:29.246 --> 34:32.246 align:middle
in financial conditions in the
form of higher interest rates

34:32.806 --> 34:33.996 align:middle
and just the sorts

34:33.996 --> 34:36.966 align:middle
of things-we're not targeting
any one or more of those things,

34:36.966 --> 34:40.516 align:middle
but financial conditions
generally should move

34:40.516 --> 34:42.496 align:middle
to a more normal level

34:42.496 --> 34:45.776 align:middle
so that-because we know the
economy no longer needs,

34:46.346 --> 34:51.026 align:middle
or wants, these very highly
accommodative, this stance,

34:51.026 --> 34:54.416 align:middle
which, you know, so it's time
to move to a more normal setting

34:54.416 --> 34:56.496 align:middle
of financial conditions,

34:56.496 --> 34:59.196 align:middle
and we do that by moving
monetary policy itself

34:59.236 --> 35:00.426 align:middle
to more normal levels.

35:01.496 --> 35:01.796 align:middle
RICH MILLER.

35:01.796 --> 35:03.996 align:middle
When you say "move to
a more normal setting"

35:03.996 --> 35:06.266 align:middle
for financial conditions,
that suggests to me

35:06.266 --> 35:08.536 align:middle
that you want financial
conditions to tighten further

35:08.536 --> 35:09.596 align:middle
from where we are now.

35:09.596 --> 35:12.306 align:middle
Am I drawing the right
inference from that?

35:12.306 --> 35:12.706 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:12.706 --> 35:13.136 align:middle
Well, yes.

35:13.136 --> 35:15.156 align:middle
So I would say, we
look at a broad range

35:15.156 --> 35:16.256 align:middle
of financial conditions.

35:16.256 --> 35:19.636 align:middle
And, of course, when we tighten
monetary policy, we do expect

35:19.636 --> 35:23.156 align:middle
that they will adjust in sync
over time with monetary policy.

35:23.156 --> 35:25.686 align:middle
It's not any particular
financial condition,

35:25.686 --> 35:27.386 align:middle
but a broad range of
financial conditions.

35:27.876 --> 35:30.836 align:middle
They will reflect to some
extent-they reflect any number

35:30.836 --> 35:31.306 align:middle
of things.

35:31.906 --> 35:36.526 align:middle
But, yes, we need our policy to
transmit to the real economy.

35:36.526 --> 35:39.816 align:middle
And it does so through financial
conditions, which means that,

35:39.816 --> 35:43.026 align:middle
as we tighten policy or remove
accommodation so that it's

35:43.026 --> 35:44.336 align:middle
at least less accommodative,

35:44.916 --> 35:47.056 align:middle
that broader financial
conditions will also be

35:47.056 --> 35:47.756 align:middle
less accommodative.

35:48.676 --> 35:50.936 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

35:51.236 --> 35:51.936 align:middle
Thank you.

35:51.936 --> 35:54.516 align:middle
Just a little housekeeping
note: Those of you

35:54.546 --> 35:57.916 align:middle
in this call may be
having some tech issues.

35:57.916 --> 36:00.826 align:middle
If so, I understand
the broadcast is coming

36:00.826 --> 36:05.186 align:middle
through clearly on
www.federalreserve.gov.

36:05.726 --> 36:07.196 align:middle
You might go there
for the audio.

36:08.376 --> 36:09.706 align:middle
And now we'll go to Jean Yung.

36:09.706 --> 36:09.936 align:middle
JEAN YUNG.

36:15.266 --> 36:16.116 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

36:16.116 --> 36:19.736 align:middle
I wanted to ask about the
balance sheet discussion you had

36:19.736 --> 36:20.536 align:middle
at this meeting.

36:20.856 --> 36:22.686 align:middle
Can you give us any
more details?

36:22.886 --> 36:27.606 align:middle
Did you discuss whether
to cap runoffs or whether

36:27.606 --> 36:29.876 align:middle
to increase those
caps over what period,

36:29.876 --> 36:30.966 align:middle
if there were any details?

36:31.776 --> 36:32.016 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:32.016 --> 36:33.156 align:middle
Yes. Thank you for asking.

36:33.406 --> 36:35.296 align:middle
So, at our meeting
today and yesterday,

36:35.296 --> 36:37.626 align:middle
we made excellent
progress toward agreeing

36:37.626 --> 36:40.636 align:middle
on the parameters of a plan
to shrink the balance sheet.

36:41.296 --> 36:43.556 align:middle
And I'd say we're now in
a position to finalize

36:43.556 --> 36:46.246 align:middle
and implement that plan so that
we'll actually begin a runoff

36:46.606 --> 36:47.536 align:middle
at a coming meeting.

36:47.536 --> 36:50.516 align:middle
And that could come as soon
as our next meeting in May.

36:50.836 --> 36:52.136 align:middle
That's not a decision
that we've made.

36:52.136 --> 36:56.106 align:middle
But I would say that that's
how well our discussions went

36:56.106 --> 36:57.336 align:middle
in the last two days.

36:58.326 --> 37:01.356 align:middle
So a couple things just to add.

37:01.356 --> 37:05.636 align:middle
We'll be mindful of the broader
financial and economic context

37:05.636 --> 37:08.556 align:middle
when we make the decision on
timing, and we always want

37:08.556 --> 37:11.166 align:middle
to use our tools to
support macroeconomic

37:11.166 --> 37:12.296 align:middle
and financial stability.

37:12.336 --> 37:13.976 align:middle
We want to avoid
adding uncertainty

37:13.976 --> 37:17.586 align:middle
to what's a highly
uncertain situation already.

37:17.586 --> 37:19.126 align:middle
So all of that will
go into the thinking

37:19.126 --> 37:20.416 align:middle
of the timing around this.

37:20.986 --> 37:24.816 align:middle
In terms of the-I
would say this.

37:24.816 --> 37:27.206 align:middle
I don't want to get too
much into the details,

37:27.206 --> 37:29.806 align:middle
because we're literally
just finalizing them.

37:30.526 --> 37:33.846 align:middle
But the framework is going to
look very familiar to people

37:33.846 --> 37:37.946 align:middle
who are familiar with the
last time we did this.

37:38.456 --> 37:42.366 align:middle
But it'll be faster
than the last time.

37:42.366 --> 37:45.126 align:middle
And, of course, it's much sooner
in a cycle than last time.

37:45.706 --> 37:48.446 align:middle
But it will look
familiar to you.

37:48.446 --> 37:50.396 align:middle
And I would also say

37:50.396 --> 37:54.036 align:middle
that there'll be-I'm sure
there'll be a more detailed

37:54.036 --> 37:58.206 align:middle
discussion of our-in the
minutes to our meeting that come

37:58.206 --> 38:00.956 align:middle
out in three weeks, where I
expect that it will lay out,

38:01.016 --> 38:02.666 align:middle
you know, pretty
much the parameters

38:02.666 --> 38:03.566 align:middle
of what we're looking at,

38:03.566 --> 38:04.946 align:middle
which I think will
look quite familiar.

38:04.946 --> 38:05.796 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

38:09.096 --> 38:09.506 align:middle
Thank you.

38:09.506 --> 38:11.936 align:middle
Let's go to Michael
McKee at Bloomberg TV.

38:11.936 --> 38:13.576 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

38:13.576 --> 38:18.026 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, since September
of 2020, you've been operating

38:18.026 --> 38:21.856 align:middle
on a monetary policy framework
that let the economy run hot

38:21.856 --> 38:24.086 align:middle
to bring unemployment down.

38:24.086 --> 38:25.116 align:middle
That seems to be over.

38:25.116 --> 38:28.256 align:middle
But I'm wondering how you
would describe your reaction

38:28.256 --> 38:29.826 align:middle
function now.

38:29.826 --> 38:32.266 align:middle
What is it that the Fed
is trying to do other

38:32.266 --> 38:33.296 align:middle
than bring inflation down?

38:33.296 --> 38:36.366 align:middle
In other words, is it, we're
going to keep raising rates

38:36.366 --> 38:38.626 align:middle
until it comes down to
an acceptable level?

38:39.976 --> 38:40.416 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:40.586 --> 38:42.886 align:middle
Yes. So I want to clear
one thing up again.

38:42.886 --> 38:47.736 align:middle
And that is that nothing in our
new framework or in the changes

38:47.736 --> 38:51.176 align:middle
that we made has
caused us to wait longer

38:51.226 --> 38:52.816 align:middle
to raise interest rates.

38:53.136 --> 38:56.776 align:middle
What we said in the framework
changes was-and this was really

38:56.776 --> 38:58.086 align:middle
a reflection of what
had happened

38:58.806 --> 39:01.346 align:middle
for the preceding couple
of decades, actually.

39:01.826 --> 39:06.156 align:middle
What we said was, if we see,
you know, low unemployment,

39:06.156 --> 39:08.356 align:middle
high employment, but
we don't see inflation,

39:09.106 --> 39:10.716 align:middle
then we're not going to
raise rates 'til we actually

39:10.716 --> 39:11.446 align:middle
see inflation.

39:11.446 --> 39:13.726 align:middle
That's what we said, and
that was the sense of it.

39:14.186 --> 39:16.816 align:middle
There was no sense in
which, if we got a burst

39:16.816 --> 39:20.266 align:middle
of really high inflation, we
would wait to raise rates.

39:20.266 --> 39:21.606 align:middle
That's simply not
in the framework.

39:21.606 --> 39:23.966 align:middle
In fact, quite the contrary.

39:23.966 --> 39:25.126 align:middle
The framework is all

39:25.126 --> 39:28.656 align:middle
about anchoring inflation
expectations at 2 percent.

39:29.276 --> 39:32.196 align:middle
So I do hear this, you know,
that the framework-really,

39:32.196 --> 39:33.716 align:middle
we can't blame the framework.

39:33.716 --> 39:37.106 align:middle
It was a sudden, unexpected
burst of inflation.

39:37.496 --> 39:40.636 align:middle
And then it was the reaction
to it, and it was what it was.

39:40.636 --> 39:46.076 align:middle
But it was not in any way caused
by or related to the framework.

39:46.566 --> 39:48.586 align:middle
So, come to today.

39:48.986 --> 39:51.386 align:middle
You know, I think
our vision on this

39:51.386 --> 39:53.096 align:middle
on the Committee is
very, very clear.

39:53.656 --> 39:56.476 align:middle
What we see is a
strong labor market.

39:56.626 --> 40:00.256 align:middle
We see a labor market with a lot
of momentum, great job creation.

40:00.846 --> 40:03.926 align:middle
And we see the underlying
economy's strong.

40:04.476 --> 40:05.966 align:middle
Balance sheets are strong.

40:06.306 --> 40:08.566 align:middle
Yes, there are threats
to growth from, you know,

40:08.566 --> 40:10.336 align:middle
from what's going on
in Eastern Europe.

40:11.016 --> 40:14.186 align:middle
And, but, nonetheless,
in the base case,

40:14.186 --> 40:16.896 align:middle
there's a pretty broad
expectation of strong growth.

40:17.536 --> 40:19.366 align:middle
But inflation is far
above our target.

40:19.926 --> 40:22.456 align:middle
And, you know, the help
we've been expecting

40:22.456 --> 40:24.266 align:middle
and other forecasters
have been expecting

40:24.266 --> 40:27.786 align:middle
from supply-side improvement,
labor force participation,

40:28.226 --> 40:31.436 align:middle
bottlenecks, all those things
getting better-it hasn't come.

40:31.956 --> 40:35.316 align:middle
And so we're looking
now to using our tools

40:35.766 --> 40:38.566 align:middle
to restore price stability, and
we're committed to doing that.

40:38.766 --> 40:40.476 align:middle
And you see that, I
think, in the Summary

40:40.476 --> 40:41.516 align:middle
of Economic Projections.

40:42.106 --> 40:44.356 align:middle
And you see that in the decision
we make, and you'll continue

40:44.356 --> 40:46.306 align:middle
to see it in the decisions
we make, going forward.

40:46.306 --> 40:46.926 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

40:47.076 --> 40:50.806 align:middle
If I could follow up by asking-I
guess what you'd call it is the

40:50.806 --> 40:52.696 align:middle
"Paul Volcker" question:

40:52.776 --> 40:55.256 align:middle
You don't think unemployment
is going to rise significantly,

40:55.256 --> 40:58.546 align:middle
but, if it does, does
that temper your desire

40:58.546 --> 41:00.496 align:middle
to keep raising rates?

41:00.686 --> 41:01.546 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:01.616 --> 41:02.666 align:middle
The goal, of course,

41:02.896 --> 41:07.926 align:middle
is to restore price stability
while also sustaining a strong

41:07.926 --> 41:08.436 align:middle
labor market.

41:08.436 --> 41:10.376 align:middle
We have a dual mandate,
and they're sort of equal.

41:11.016 --> 41:13.426 align:middle
But, as I said earlier,
you know,

41:13.556 --> 41:17.676 align:middle
price stability is
an essential goal.

41:17.676 --> 41:21.066 align:middle
In fact, it's a precondition,
really, for achieving the kind

41:21.066 --> 41:24.136 align:middle
of labor market that we
want, which is a strong

41:24.136 --> 41:25.796 align:middle
and sustained labor market.

41:25.796 --> 41:28.416 align:middle
We saw the benefits
of a long expansion,

41:28.786 --> 41:30.236 align:middle
a sustained labor market.

41:30.236 --> 41:34.796 align:middle
It pulled people back in, and
there were really no imbalances

41:34.796 --> 41:36.936 align:middle
in the economy that
threatened the long expansion.

41:36.936 --> 41:38.796 align:middle
It, just, the pandemic arrived;

41:38.796 --> 41:41.086 align:middle
it was just a completely
exogenous event.

41:41.766 --> 41:43.526 align:middle
So that's how we're
thinking about it.

41:43.526 --> 41:48.516 align:middle
We, of course, want to achieve,
you know, price stability

41:48.516 --> 41:50.006 align:middle
with a strong labor market.

41:50.196 --> 41:52.356 align:middle
But we do understand
also that, really,

41:52.356 --> 41:54.066 align:middle
you can't have maximum
employment

41:54.326 --> 41:56.346 align:middle
for any sustained period
without price stability.

41:56.346 --> 41:59.946 align:middle
So we need to focus on price
stability particularly,

41:59.976 --> 42:02.166 align:middle
because the labor
market is so strong

42:02.546 --> 42:03.866 align:middle
and the economy is so strong.

42:03.866 --> 42:07.236 align:middle
We feel like the economy can
handle tighter monetary policy.

42:07.236 --> 42:08.246 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

42:10.066 --> 42:10.446 align:middle
Thank you.

42:10.446 --> 42:12.236 align:middle
Let's go to Brian
Cheung at Yahoo.

42:12.236 --> 42:14.106 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

42:14.106 --> 42:14.676 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

42:14.676 --> 42:16.666 align:middle
Hopefully no tech issues
here on this front.

42:16.666 --> 42:18.756 align:middle
Wanted to ask just kind
of the broad question

42:18.756 --> 42:22.056 align:middle
about how you are communicating
what the Fed's doing here today

42:22.056 --> 42:23.276 align:middle
to the average American

42:23.276 --> 42:25.216 align:middle
who might not be
reading the dot plots

42:25.216 --> 42:26.856 align:middle
or understanding
what the SEP is.

42:26.856 --> 42:30.356 align:middle
How is the 25 basis point hike
today and then the signaling

42:30.356 --> 42:33.296 align:middle
on future Fed policy going
to address the inflation

42:33.296 --> 42:35.056 align:middle
that they're feeling at the
stores on a daily basis?

42:35.056 --> 42:35.676 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:35.676 --> 42:38.886 align:middle
Sure. So I guess I'd start
by just assuring everyone

42:39.446 --> 42:42.086 align:middle
that we're fully committed to
bringing inflation back down

42:42.266 --> 42:44.276 align:middle
and also sustaining
the economic expansion.

42:44.736 --> 42:46.996 align:middle
We do understand that
these higher prices,

42:46.996 --> 42:49.496 align:middle
no matter what the
source, have real effects

42:49.496 --> 42:50.746 align:middle
on people's well-being.

42:51.196 --> 42:53.486 align:middle
And, really, high inflation
takes a toll on everyone.

42:53.536 --> 42:57.556 align:middle
But it's really, especially,
on people who use most

42:57.556 --> 43:00.096 align:middle
of their income to buy
essentials like food, housing,

43:00.096 --> 43:03.816 align:middle
and transportation, where-I
mean, we've all seen charts

43:03.816 --> 43:05.916 align:middle
that show, if you're a
middle-income person,

43:06.236 --> 43:09.616 align:middle
you've got room to
absorb some inflation.

43:10.206 --> 43:12.206 align:middle
If you're at the lower end
of the income spectrum,

43:12.256 --> 43:14.146 align:middle
it's very hard because
you're spending most

43:14.146 --> 43:17.196 align:middle
of your money already
on necessities,

43:17.196 --> 43:18.436 align:middle
and the price is going up.

43:18.506 --> 43:21.966 align:middle
But it's punishing for everyone.

43:21.966 --> 43:24.876 align:middle
So it has been a difficult
time for the economy.

43:24.936 --> 43:28.206 align:middle
But we do anticipate that
inflation will move back down,

43:28.206 --> 43:29.506 align:middle
as I mentioned earlier.

43:30.186 --> 43:33.056 align:middle
It may take longer than
we like, but I'm confident

43:33.056 --> 43:35.106 align:middle
that we'll use our tools
to bring inflation down.

43:35.616 --> 43:36.916 align:middle
You asked about rates.

43:36.916 --> 43:39.236 align:middle
So the way that works,
I would explain, is,

43:39.646 --> 43:42.496 align:middle
as we raise interest rates,
that should gradually slow

43:42.496 --> 43:44.846 align:middle
down demand for the
interest-sensitive parts

43:44.846 --> 43:45.506 align:middle
of the economy.

43:46.256 --> 43:49.606 align:middle
And so what we would see
is demand slowing down,

43:49.606 --> 43:52.136 align:middle
but just enough so that it's
better matched with supply.

43:52.596 --> 43:55.996 align:middle
And that brings-that will
bring inflation down over time.

43:56.146 --> 43:56.796 align:middle
That's our plan.

43:56.936 --> 43:57.536 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

44:00.166 --> 44:00.586 align:middle
Thank you.

44:00.586 --> 44:02.616 align:middle
Let's go to Jo Ling
Kent at NBC News.

44:02.616 --> 44:03.456 align:middle
JO LING KENT.

44:06.746 --> 44:07.746 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

44:07.746 --> 44:10.456 align:middle
Thank you so much for taking
my question during this today.

44:10.456 --> 44:12.886 align:middle
My question is a follow-up
to what Brian just asked.

44:12.886 --> 44:14.766 align:middle
What is your message
to consumers out there

44:14.766 --> 44:17.396 align:middle
who can no longer
afford the basics due

44:17.396 --> 44:18.876 align:middle
to this high inflation?

44:19.436 --> 44:20.426 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:20.606 --> 44:22.036 align:middle
Well, that is-yes, indeed.

44:22.036 --> 44:24.566 align:middle
I mean, as I just
said, you know,

44:24.566 --> 44:29.136 align:middle
I think we do understand very
much, and we very much take

44:29.136 --> 44:30.946 align:middle
to heart, that it's
our obligation

44:31.476 --> 44:33.656 align:middle
to restore price stability.

44:34.366 --> 44:38.246 align:middle
And, you know, we've had price
stability for a very long time

44:38.246 --> 44:40.146 align:middle
and maybe come to
take it for granted.

44:40.186 --> 44:41.846 align:middle
But now we see the pain.

44:41.846 --> 44:45.716 align:middle
I'm old enough to remember what
very high inflation was like.

44:46.316 --> 44:49.486 align:middle
And, you know, we're strongly
committed as a Committee

44:49.856 --> 44:53.086 align:middle
to not allowing this higher
inflation to become entrenched

44:53.556 --> 44:56.276 align:middle
and to use our tools
to bring inflation back

44:56.366 --> 44:58.366 align:middle
down to more normal levels,

44:58.366 --> 45:01.326 align:middle
which-our target is
2 percent inflation.

45:01.816 --> 45:04.246 align:middle
So we will do that.

45:04.246 --> 45:06.626 align:middle
And I just would want
people to understand that,

45:06.626 --> 45:08.026 align:middle
and that's-but the
way we do that is

45:08.026 --> 45:10.386 align:middle
by raising interest rates and
by shrinking our balance sheet.

45:10.386 --> 45:14.236 align:middle
And so financial conditions
will become, at the margin,

45:14.236 --> 45:17.226 align:middle
less supportive of various
kinds of economic activity.

45:17.226 --> 45:18.586 align:middle
That will slow the economy,

45:19.306 --> 45:22.056 align:middle
while also allowing the labor
market to remain very strong.

45:22.056 --> 45:24.196 align:middle
And, you know, the good
news is, the economy

45:24.196 --> 45:26.646 align:middle
and the labor market
are quite strong.

45:27.336 --> 45:30.526 align:middle
And that means the
economy, we think,

45:30.586 --> 45:32.566 align:middle
can handle interest
rate increases.

45:32.676 --> 45:33.296 align:middle
JO LING KENT.

45:34.466 --> 45:36.606 align:middle
And as a quick follow-up to
that, you know, obviously,

45:36.606 --> 45:39.526 align:middle
the Federal Reserve walking
this very complicated fine line,

45:39.526 --> 45:41.406 align:middle
trying to avoid a recession.

45:41.406 --> 45:43.926 align:middle
For the consumers out there who
are worried about their jobs

45:43.926 --> 45:46.376 align:middle
in a possible recession,
what do you say to that?

45:46.596 --> 45:47.886 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

45:48.076 --> 45:50.946 align:middle
Well, I, you know, I say
that our intention is

45:52.136 --> 45:54.456 align:middle
to bring inflation
back down to 2 percent,

45:54.456 --> 45:56.826 align:middle
while still sustaining
a strong labor market

45:57.316 --> 45:58.526 align:middle
and that the economy
is very strong.

45:58.526 --> 46:01.496 align:middle
If you look at where
forecasts are,

46:01.806 --> 46:07.406 align:middle
people are forecasting growth
that's strong within the context

46:07.406 --> 46:09.316 align:middle
of U.S. potential
economic output.

46:10.406 --> 46:12.386 align:middle
So-and we expect
that to continue.

46:12.916 --> 46:16.016 align:middle
And to the extent the data come
in different, then, of course,

46:16.016 --> 46:17.176 align:middle
our policy will adapt.

46:17.746 --> 46:20.336 align:middle
But we do believe that our
policy is the appropriate one

46:20.336 --> 46:21.796 align:middle
for this forecast,
and we believe

46:21.796 --> 46:23.046 align:middle
that we can bring
down inflation.

46:23.436 --> 46:24.236 align:middle
We believe that we can do

46:24.236 --> 46:27.626 align:middle
so while sustaining a
strong labor market.

46:27.626 --> 46:30.616 align:middle
The labor market
is-it's not strong

46:30.616 --> 46:32.506 align:middle
in the ordinary sense
of the word.

46:32.586 --> 46:36.836 align:middle
We have not seen a labor market
where there are 1.7 job openings

46:36.836 --> 46:40.886 align:middle
for every unemployed
person or where there,

46:40.886 --> 46:45.166 align:middle
if you add job openings
to those who are employed,

46:45.596 --> 46:48.206 align:middle
that's actually substantially
a larger number than the size

46:48.206 --> 46:50.326 align:middle
of the workforce, than
the number of people

46:50.326 --> 46:51.826 align:middle
who actually count
themselves as in the workforce.

46:51.826 --> 46:55.876 align:middle
So this is a situation where
demand is higher than supply.

46:56.356 --> 46:58.946 align:middle
And when that happens,
prices go up.

46:58.946 --> 47:01.856 align:middle
And they-so we need
to use our tools

47:02.756 --> 47:04.246 align:middle
to move supply and demand back.

47:04.246 --> 47:07.366 align:middle
And we don't think we
need to do this alone.

47:07.366 --> 47:09.406 align:middle
There will be other
factors helping that happen.

47:09.616 --> 47:13.006 align:middle
But we certainly are prepared
to use our tools-and we will.

47:13.006 --> 47:13.416 align:middle
JO LING KENT.

47:14.276 --> 47:15.456 align:middle
Thank you.

47:15.456 --> 47:16.646 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

47:16.646 --> 47:17.006 align:middle
Thank you.

47:17.006 --> 47:19.156 align:middle
Let's go to Simon
at the Economist.

47:19.276 --> 47:21.036 align:middle
SIMON RABINOVITCH.

47:21.116 --> 47:22.136 align:middle
Hi. Thank you, Michelle.

47:22.136 --> 47:23.166 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

47:23.306 --> 47:26.156 align:middle
Sorry to take you away from
inflation for one minute.

47:26.156 --> 47:29.836 align:middle
May I ask about the sanctions
on Russia and, specifically,

47:29.836 --> 47:31.796 align:middle
the freezing of the
central bank assets?

47:31.796 --> 47:34.926 align:middle
Of course, that raises a similar
risk for other sovereigns

47:34.926 --> 47:37.976 align:middle
around the world and their
biggest companies, potentially.

47:37.976 --> 47:40.696 align:middle
Any concerns about,
in the long term,

47:40.976 --> 47:43.596 align:middle
how this might affect
the dollar's status

47:43.596 --> 47:45.556 align:middle
as the preeminent
global reserve currency?

47:45.866 --> 47:48.316 align:middle
And in the past couple
of weeks, have you had

47:48.316 --> 47:50.816 align:middle
to deliver any kinds
of reassurance

47:50.816 --> 47:52.626 align:middle
to other central
bankers around the world?

47:52.626 --> 47:53.576 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:53.916 --> 47:55.636 align:middle
Well, so, of course,
central bankers

47:55.636 --> 47:57.486 align:middle
around the world
are generally very

47:57.616 --> 47:59.216 align:middle
in favor of these sanctions.

47:59.566 --> 48:02.496 align:middle
But let me say this: Sanctions
are really the business

48:02.496 --> 48:04.926 align:middle
of the elected government,
and that's true everywhere.

48:04.926 --> 48:07.986 align:middle
So the Administration and
the Treasury Department,

48:07.986 --> 48:11.746 align:middle
in particular, and other
agencies-they create

48:11.746 --> 48:12.416 align:middle
these sanctions.

48:12.946 --> 48:15.506 align:middle
We're there to provide technical
expertise, but they're not,

48:15.506 --> 48:16.686 align:middle
it's not our decisions.

48:16.996 --> 48:20.506 align:middle
And so I'm reluctant to comment

48:20.506 --> 48:23.536 align:middle
on sanctions really
much because, again,

48:23.536 --> 48:24.626 align:middle
they're not for us [to decide].

48:24.626 --> 48:26.366 align:middle
We have a very specific mandate.

48:26.366 --> 48:28.966 align:middle
And these are really
the province

48:28.966 --> 48:31.146 align:middle
of elected governments,
as I mentioned.

48:31.856 --> 48:33.386 align:middle
So I'd have to leave it at that.

48:33.386 --> 48:34.126 align:middle
Sorry. MICHELLE SMITH.

48:36.276 --> 48:36.686 align:middle
Thank you.

48:36.686 --> 48:39.746 align:middle
Let's go to Nancy
Marshall-Genzer at Marketplace.

48:39.746 --> 48:41.296 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

48:41.386 --> 48:42.426 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

48:42.426 --> 48:44.686 align:middle
Thanks for taking the question.

48:44.766 --> 48:47.496 align:middle
You've been talking a lot
about rising wages, which,

48:47.526 --> 48:48.496 align:middle
on the one hand,
is a great thing,

48:48.496 --> 48:50.056 align:middle
but are we possibly
seeing the beginning

48:50.056 --> 48:50.846 align:middle
of a wage-price spiral?

48:50.956 --> 48:51.236 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:51.406 --> 48:53.546 align:middle
So the way I would say it
is this: We, first of all,

48:53.546 --> 48:54.606 align:middle
I would agree with the premise

48:54.606 --> 48:56.486 align:middle
that wages moving
up is a great thing.

48:56.486 --> 48:59.066 align:middle
You know, that's
how the standard

48:59.066 --> 49:00.546 align:middle
of living rises over time.

49:00.546 --> 49:02.726 align:middle
And, generally, it's
driven over long periods

49:02.726 --> 49:03.946 align:middle
by rising productivity.

49:04.686 --> 49:08.896 align:middle
But what we have now,
if you look at these,

49:08.896 --> 49:12.606 align:middle
the wage increases that we
have, we look at a-we're blessed

49:12.606 --> 49:14.886 align:middle
with a range of measures
of wages

49:14.886 --> 49:17.046 align:middle
that all measure
different things.

49:17.046 --> 49:19.396 align:middle
But right now, they're all
showing the same thing,

49:19.396 --> 49:21.336 align:middle
which is that the
increases-not the levels,

49:21.336 --> 49:24.796 align:middle
but the increases-are running
at levels that are well

49:24.796 --> 49:27.226 align:middle
above what would be consistent

49:27.226 --> 49:30.506 align:middle
with 2 percent inflation,
our goal, over time.

49:31.316 --> 49:33.946 align:middle
And that may be-we don't
know how persistent

49:33.946 --> 49:35.086 align:middle
that phenomenon will be.

49:35.506 --> 49:36.586 align:middle
It's very hard to say.

49:37.316 --> 49:40.646 align:middle
And that's really, I think,
the sense of your question

49:40.646 --> 49:43.126 align:middle
about a wage-price spiral; is
that something that's going

49:43.126 --> 49:46.896 align:middle
to start happening and become
entrenched in the system?

49:46.896 --> 49:49.326 align:middle
We don't see that.

49:49.326 --> 49:52.156 align:middle
You can see, for
example, in some sectors

49:52.156 --> 49:56.206 align:middle
that got very high wage
increases early on,

49:56.606 --> 49:59.486 align:middle
those wage increases looked
like they may have slowed

49:59.486 --> 50:01.016 align:middle
down to a normal level.

50:01.336 --> 50:06.956 align:middle
But it comes back to, you know,
what I'm saying here-which is,

50:07.506 --> 50:11.446 align:middle
there's a misalignment
of demand and supply,

50:11.446 --> 50:12.886 align:middle
particularly in the
labor market.

50:12.886 --> 50:17.056 align:middle
And that is leading to
wages moving up at ways

50:17.056 --> 50:21.186 align:middle
that are not consistent with
2 percent inflation over time.

50:21.916 --> 50:26.426 align:middle
And so we need to use
our tools to, you know,

50:26.426 --> 50:28.416 align:middle
guide inflation back
down to 2 percent.

50:29.026 --> 50:30.546 align:middle
And that would be in the context

50:30.546 --> 50:33.196 align:middle
of an extraordinarily
strong labor market.

50:33.196 --> 50:37.236 align:middle
We think this labor market
can handle, as I mentioned,

50:37.746 --> 50:39.336 align:middle
tighter monetary policy.

50:39.646 --> 50:42.246 align:middle
And the overall economy
can as well.

50:42.246 --> 50:48.436 align:middle
But, yes, wages are moving
up faster than is consistent

50:48.436 --> 50:51.516 align:middle
with 2 percent inflation, but
it's good to see that moving up.

50:51.516 --> 50:54.866 align:middle
But it wouldn't be sustainable
over too long of a period

50:54.866 --> 50:56.876 align:middle
to see them moving
up that much higher.

50:57.236 --> 50:58.826 align:middle
And that's because
of this misalignment

50:58.826 --> 51:00.056 align:middle
between supply and demand.

51:00.306 --> 51:02.006 align:middle
We expect to get
more labor supply.

51:02.396 --> 51:03.526 align:middle
We did last time.

51:03.526 --> 51:05.806 align:middle
We got more than we expected
during the last cycle.

51:06.216 --> 51:08.666 align:middle
This time, we've gotten
much less than expected.

51:08.666 --> 51:10.276 align:middle
So it's not easy to
predict these things.

51:10.776 --> 51:12.856 align:middle
But we do expect that we'll
get people coming back

51:12.856 --> 51:14.376 align:middle
in the labor market,
particularly

51:14.916 --> 51:17.776 align:middle
as COVID becomes less
and less of a factor

51:17.896 --> 51:22.296 align:middle
in many people's lives,
something we all wish.

51:22.916 --> 51:24.696 align:middle
But, so that's how
we think about it.

51:24.696 --> 51:25.536 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

51:27.156 --> 51:27.566 align:middle
Thank you.

51:27.566 --> 51:30.566 align:middle
For the last question, we'll go
to Don Lee at the L.A. Times.

51:30.606 --> 51:32.506 align:middle
DON LEE. Hi, Chair Powell.

51:32.506 --> 51:36.896 align:middle
I think you said to the Senate
earlier this month that,

51:37.036 --> 51:39.976 align:middle
in hindsight, the Fed
should have moved earlier.

51:40.516 --> 51:43.476 align:middle
And it sounds like today

51:43.476 --> 51:46.656 align:middle
that you don't think
that the Fed is late.

51:46.656 --> 51:50.376 align:middle
And, just wanted to get
your clarification on that.

51:50.376 --> 51:54.306 align:middle
And if it is, if you still
think that the Fed is

51:54.306 --> 51:58.246 align:middle
"behind the curve," how much
"behind the curve" is it?

51:58.246 --> 51:59.686 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

51:59.686 --> 52:03.416 align:middle
Right. So we are not-we
don't have the luxury

52:03.416 --> 52:07.096 align:middle
of 20/20 hindsight in actually
implementing real-time decisions

52:07.096 --> 52:07.786 align:middle
in the world.

52:08.326 --> 52:13.896 align:middle
So, you know, so the question
is-the right question is,

52:13.966 --> 52:15.296 align:middle
did you make the
right decisions based

52:15.296 --> 52:16.326 align:middle
on what you knew at the time?

52:16.786 --> 52:18.266 align:middle
But that's not the
question I was answering,

52:18.266 --> 52:19.926 align:middle
which is knowing
what you know now.

52:20.226 --> 52:22.946 align:middle
So I think if we knew now-of
course, if we knew now

52:22.946 --> 52:26.526 align:middle
that these supply
blockages, really,

52:26.526 --> 52:29.376 align:middle
and the inflation resulting
from them in collision with,

52:29.576 --> 52:30.716 align:middle
you know, very strong demand,

52:30.716 --> 52:33.236 align:middle
if we knew that that was
what was going to happen,

52:33.576 --> 52:34.606 align:middle
then in hindsight, yes.

52:34.606 --> 52:37.156 align:middle
It would have been
appropriate to move earlier.

52:37.886 --> 52:39.146 align:middle
Obviously, it would be.

52:39.146 --> 52:41.476 align:middle
But, again, we don't
have that luxury.

52:42.006 --> 52:44.926 align:middle
And then, so, but that's
a separate question

52:44.926 --> 52:48.176 align:middle
from your other question,
which is "behind the curve."

52:48.176 --> 52:49.936 align:middle
And, you know, I
don't have the luxury

52:50.006 --> 52:52.526 align:middle
of looking at it that way.

52:52.526 --> 52:58.896 align:middle
You know, we are, we have our
tools-powerful tools-and the

52:58.896 --> 53:02.176 align:middle
Committee is very
focused on using them.

53:02.176 --> 53:03.996 align:middle
We're acutely aware of the need

53:03.996 --> 53:07.616 align:middle
to restore price stability while
keeping a strong labor market.

53:07.926 --> 53:10.426 align:middle
And what I saw today
was a Committee

53:10.426 --> 53:14.566 align:middle
that is strongly committed
to achieving price stability,

53:15.146 --> 53:18.396 align:middle
in particular, and prepared
to use our tools to do that.

53:19.056 --> 53:21.436 align:middle
We're not going to let high
inflation become entrenched.

53:21.436 --> 53:23.206 align:middle
The costs of that
would be too high.

53:24.046 --> 53:27.046 align:middle
And we're not going to wait so
long that we have to do that.

53:27.306 --> 53:31.786 align:middle
No one wants to have to really
put restrictive monetary policy

53:31.786 --> 53:34.236 align:middle
on, in order to get
inflation back down.

53:34.236 --> 53:39.026 align:middle
So, frankly, the need is
one of getting back up,

53:39.116 --> 53:42.686 align:middle
getting rates back up to more
neutral levels as quickly

53:42.686 --> 53:46.786 align:middle
as we practicably can and
then moving beyond that,

53:46.886 --> 53:48.356 align:middle
if that turns out
to be appropriate.

53:48.356 --> 53:52.966 align:middle
And, as you can see, it is
appropriate in the sense that,

53:52.966 --> 53:55.026 align:middle
[in] people's [contributions
to the] SEPs, they do write

53:55.026 --> 53:58.786 align:middle
down levels of interest rates
that are above their estimate

53:58.786 --> 54:00.446 align:middle
of the longer-run neutral rate.

54:00.536 --> 54:03.746 align:middle
And there's also a range of
estimates, too, as you will see

54:03.746 --> 54:05.406 align:middle
if you look at the
details of the SEP.

54:05.626 --> 54:07.036 align:middle
But thanks for your question.

54:07.036 --> 54:09.286 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

54:09.286 --> 54:10.616 align:middle
Okay. Thank you all.

54:10.616 --> 54:11.546 align:middle
Thanks, Mr. Chair.

54:11.546 --> 54:12.096 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

54:12.096 --> 54:13.046 align:middle
Thank you.

